00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:24,753
and also essentially just not an efficient workflow, right?

532
00:21:25,650 --> 00:21:28,770
There's also implications of data integrity,

533
00:21:28,770 --> 00:21:32,343
and that really impacts our IR office as well.

534
00:21:33,720 --> 00:21:36,090
So, one of the things that we did...

535
00:21:36,090 --> 00:21:40,650
And luckily, we actually met N2N at a different conference

536
00:21:40,650 --> 00:21:43,260
sometime last year or about last year,

537
00:21:43,260 --> 00:21:47,793
and in talking about these sorts of challenges, we thought,

538
00:21:50,497 --> 00:21:53,313
"Let's look at N2N," essentially.

539
00:21:55,391 --> 00:21:57,420
What N2N has done for us

540
00:21:57,420 --> 00:22:01,080
is better streamline our process, right?

541
00:22:01,080 --> 00:22:03,420
So when we get these stacks of applications

542
00:22:03,420 --> 00:22:05,160
that come in on a weekly basis,

543
00:22:05,160 --> 00:22:07,980
we scan it up to our N2N platform.

544
00:22:07,980 --> 00:22:11,880
From there, they then extract these handwritten data

545
00:22:11,880 --> 00:22:15,003
into our SIS system directly.

546
00:22:16,440 --> 00:22:17,850
When they're able to do that,

547
00:22:17,850 --> 00:22:19,350
when the staff opens it up,

548
00:22:19,350 --> 00:22:21,480
we can compare the scanned documents

549
00:22:21,480 --> 00:22:24,000
with what's populated,

550
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,160
and there's actually an accuracy meter.

551
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:28,953
So say, for example, the name is like 90% accurate.

552
00:22:29,850 --> 00:22:32,280
Generally, 90% accurate, in my mind, is pretty accurate.

553
00:22:32,280 --> 00:22:34,890
If it's like 85 or 80 or below,

554
00:22:34,890 --> 00:22:36,450
we double-check those, right?

555
00:22:36,450 --> 00:22:39,600
And staff essentially goes and just compares it, checks it,

556
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:44,600
and finally, like, approve the information.

557
00:22:46,470 --> 00:22:49,950
We went live about like three to four weeks ago,

558
00:22:49,950 --> 00:22:51,570
and thus far, it's been the most exciting thing

559
00:22:51,570 --> 00:22:52,890
for our staff

560
00:22:52,890 --> 00:22:55,620
because, again, it really has cut down the manual process

561
00:22:55,620 --> 00:22:57,917
of these applications that come in.

562
00:22:57,917 --> 00:23:01,620
And so we're doing it for our Rising Scholars population

563
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:04,050
and also for our Fire Science population.

564
00:23:04,050 --> 00:23:06,900
And I must say, for our Fire Science population,

565
00:23:06,900 --> 00:23:08,700
you would think, "Well, they are firefighters.

566
00:23:08,700 --> 00:23:10,710
You know, they have open access to internet.

567
00:23:10,710 --> 00:23:12,390
They can do this in CCApply";

568
00:23:12,390 --> 00:23:16,260
however, the preference is paper, right?

569
00:23:16,260 --> 00:23:17,700
And we have to be good stewards,

570
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:19,860
and we have to provide excellent customer service

571
00:23:19,860 --> 00:23:22,890
'cause we're talking about at least 1,000 FTES.

572
00:23:22,890 --> 00:23:26,730
If we lose Fire Science, that's impacting jobs, right?

573
00:23:26,730 --> 00:23:29,817
And so they have been very vocal about "you know what?

574
00:23:29,817 --> 00:23:32,520
Our firefighters are good with paper applications.

575
00:23:32,520 --> 00:23:33,353
They're better with that."

576
00:23:33,353 --> 00:23:36,750
And so we have continued to use paper applications for them.

577
00:23:36,750 --> 00:23:38,220
But again, I think our...

578
00:23:38,220 --> 00:23:41,490
And finding N2N as a solution to process this

579
00:23:41,490 --> 00:23:43,680
has been night and day for us.

580
00:23:43,680 --> 00:23:45,390
And so we're just really thrilled

581
00:23:45,390 --> 00:23:47,313
to really showcase that here.

582
00:23:48,930 --> 00:23:49,770
- Thank you, Ger.

583
00:23:49,770 --> 00:23:51,840
I think this is a great example again

584
00:23:51,840 --> 00:23:54,630
of how are we utilizing AI

585
00:23:54,630 --> 00:23:57,570
to still meet students where they are,

586
00:23:57,570 --> 00:24:01,890
because if they don't have the resources they're gone

587
00:24:01,890 --> 00:24:03,900
and we still want to impact them.

588
00:24:03,900 --> 00:24:05,490
And so meeting them where they are

589
00:24:05,490 --> 00:24:08,700
but still finding solutions for our employees

590
00:24:08,700 --> 00:24:09,840
is really important.

591
00:24:09,840 --> 00:24:11,880
And Allison, I believe

592
00:24:11,880 --> 00:24:15,000
you, also at Santiago Canyon, had some issues

593
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,070
with manual entry and delays.

594
00:24:17,070 --> 00:24:18,690
And so I'll hand it to you.

595
00:24:18,690 --> 00:24:20,673
I'll be the remote right now.

596
00:24:22,080 --> 00:24:25,740
- I just wanna say to my fellow panelists and the audience,

597
00:24:25,740 --> 00:24:28,770
this is my clicker that I offered to share,

598
00:24:28,770 --> 00:24:31,773
and obviously, that was a bad idea, so sorry.

599
00:24:31,773 --> 00:24:34,350
Thought I was being helpful, but it's okay.

600
00:24:34,350 --> 00:24:37,170
We can just keep with our normal process.

601
00:24:37,170 --> 00:24:39,030
Hello, everyone, my name is Allison Maricic.

602
00:24:39,030 --> 00:24:41,400
I'm the counselor/coordinator

603
00:24:41,400 --> 00:24:44,370
of Apprenticeship at Santiago Canyon College.

604
00:24:44,370 --> 00:24:46,320
So, when I came into my role...

605
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:47,640
My position is very new,

606
00:24:47,640 --> 00:24:50,430
so I came into my position last year,

607
00:24:50,430 --> 00:24:52,290
and one of the things that I noticed

608
00:24:52,290 --> 00:24:54,630
as I was kind of taking the lay of the land,

609
00:24:54,630 --> 00:24:56,190
as we talked about earlier,

610
00:24:56,190 --> 00:24:58,650
is where were some of the gaps

611
00:24:58,650 --> 00:25:03,330
in terms of how people are evaluated in terms of our numbers

612
00:25:03,330 --> 00:25:05,100
and how we're serving our students.

613
00:25:05,100 --> 00:25:07,020
So one of the biggest gaps that I found

614
00:25:07,020 --> 00:25:09,570
was that our apprenticeship students,

615
00:25:09,570 --> 00:25:12,330
which encompasses a big part

616
00:25:12,330 --> 00:25:13,800
of our population as a campus...

617
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:15,480
We're a big apprenticeship program

618
00:25:15,480 --> 00:25:16,500
at Santiago Canyon College,

619
00:25:16,500 --> 00:25:18,750
for those of you that don't know,

620
00:25:18,750 --> 00:25:20,910
and our students' numbers

621
00:25:20,910 --> 00:25:22,260
when we look at our graduation rates

622
00:25:22,260 --> 00:25:25,950
weren't reflecting what our students were actually doing

623
00:25:25,950 --> 00:25:29,220
at their training centers and journeying out, right?

624
00:25:29,220 --> 00:25:31,830
So I was like, "Well, why isn't this happening?

625
00:25:31,830 --> 00:25:34,560
Why don't we see these reflections of that?"

626
00:25:34,560 --> 00:25:35,970
And one of the things that we found

627
00:25:35,970 --> 00:25:39,570
was that the manual process, right,

628
00:25:39,570 --> 00:25:41,610
of going into evaluating

629
00:25:41,610 --> 00:25:45,810
the graduation and the completion of the students

630
00:25:45,810 --> 00:25:48,120
was very time-consuming.

631
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:51,273
Because of the nature of traditional trades,

632
00:25:52,470 --> 00:25:55,170
the training is typically done offsite, right?

633
00:25:55,170 --> 00:25:56,280
It's not done at our college.

634
00:25:56,280 --> 00:25:57,990
It's done through the training centers

635
00:25:57,990 --> 00:25:59,670
of the different locations:

636
00:25:59,670 --> 00:26:02,010
carpentry, electrician, for example.

637
00:26:02,010 --> 00:26:04,710
And one of the things that happens

638
00:26:04,710 --> 00:26:07,890
is that that information is sent

639
00:26:07,890 --> 00:26:10,380
through a very old-school process

640
00:26:10,380 --> 00:26:11,670
of how the training trust like

641
00:26:11,670 --> 00:26:13,170
to send their information over to us.

642
00:26:13,170 --> 00:26:16,350
And then we have to manually input that information.

643
00:26:16,350 --> 00:26:18,180
And then from there, at the end,

644
00:26:18,180 --> 00:26:19,980
students end up with two transcripts,

645
00:26:19,980 --> 00:26:23,250
one from the training trust and then one from our college,

646
00:26:23,250 --> 00:26:26,400
where our transcript at the end wasn't always reflective

647
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:29,370
of what they actually did in their training,

648
00:26:29,370 --> 00:26:31,047
so we had to find a solution.

649
00:26:31,047 --> 00:26:33,840
And so one of the things that we found

650
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:36,030
was that my counselor and my colleagues were

651
00:26:36,030 --> 00:26:38,340
spending hours to, sometimes, weeks

652
00:26:38,340 --> 00:26:40,620
processing one student,

653
00:26:40,620 --> 00:26:43,080
so when we have thousands...

654
00:26:43,080 --> 00:26:44,610
Right now, we have a population

655
00:26:44,610 --> 00:26:49,050
of a little over 5,000 current apprentices at our campus.

656
00:26:49,050 --> 00:26:50,910
Those students are graduating all of the time,

657
00:26:50,910 --> 00:26:53,040
and our numbers were not reflecting that.

658
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:56,640
So luckily, we were able to connect and collaborate with N2N

659
00:26:56,640 --> 00:26:58,860
and talk about this particular issue

660
00:26:58,860 --> 00:27:01,750
and what we can do to help kind of bring forward

661
00:27:02,610 --> 00:27:04,560
their platform and their model

662
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:07,740
to help us kind of break through these barriers,

663
00:27:07,740 --> 00:27:10,380
these, like, manual processing bottlenecks,

664
00:27:10,380 --> 00:27:13,143
very similar things to what my colleagues have shared,

665
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:16,800
so that our students can get the certificates

666
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:18,450
that they deserve, right?

667
00:27:18,450 --> 00:27:20,940
They are eligible for a certificate of achievement,

668
00:27:20,940 --> 00:27:22,260
and they also have the ability

669
00:27:22,260 --> 00:27:24,060
to get an associate's degree

670
00:27:24,060 --> 00:27:25,230
in their apprenticeship programs

671
00:27:25,230 --> 00:27:26,970
if they have general education,

672
00:27:26,970 --> 00:27:29,160
and the students were just not getting the right access,

673
00:27:29,160 --> 00:27:31,320
even though they had completed the programs.

674
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:34,860
So we were able to first kind of see...

675
00:27:34,860 --> 00:27:36,000
Well, what can we do?

676
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:37,830
How can we kind of break down this process?

677
00:27:37,830 --> 00:27:41,400
Well, in our collaboration, our proposed solution...

678
00:27:41,400 --> 00:27:44,010
I'll say that, out of, I think, everyone here,

679
00:27:44,010 --> 00:27:45,570
we have not yet gone live.

680
00:27:45,570 --> 00:27:48,000
So, we are still kind of in the planning stages,

681
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:52,080
but we're very close to being able to kind of test this out.

682
00:27:52,080 --> 00:27:54,570
But we're really excited about what we see so far

683
00:27:54,570 --> 00:27:56,940
in terms of what we will be able to do

684
00:27:56,940 --> 00:27:59,040
with this integration,

685
00:27:59,040 --> 00:28:01,980
and I think this has a lot of amazing implications

686
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:03,210
for many of your colleges

687
00:28:03,210 --> 00:28:05,760
if we think about just transcript evaluation

688
00:28:05,760 --> 00:28:06,900
as a whole, right?

689
00:28:06,900 --> 00:28:09,660
All of us get other college transcripts,

690
00:28:09,660 --> 00:28:11,430
whether you have apprenticeship programs or not,

691
00:28:11,430 --> 00:28:14,820
that you have to figure out how they fit into your programs,

692
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:18,000
so this has amazing implications for everyone.

693
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:19,950
But for us, what the...

694
00:28:19,950 --> 00:28:20,820
One of the great things

695
00:28:20,820 --> 00:28:24,120
that the model and the platform is able to bring to us

696
00:28:24,120 --> 00:28:26,430
is that it brings everything to one place.

697
00:28:26,430 --> 00:28:28,560
So one of the things that is difficult

698
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:31,830
is that we, again, have these outside transcripts.

699
00:28:31,830 --> 00:28:35,280
We have, you know, missing information.

700
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:36,230
And so it's able to

701
00:28:39,492 --> 00:28:41,640
bring it all forward into one place

702
00:28:41,640 --> 00:28:44,550
so we can see all of the information together.

703
00:28:44,550 --> 00:28:47,610
One of the other great things that this has helped us do

704
00:28:47,610 --> 00:28:50,160
is kind of look at our internal processes

705
00:28:50,160 --> 00:28:52,650
is how can we better support our students

706
00:28:52,650 --> 00:28:54,540
and how can we break down some of the barriers

707
00:28:54,540 --> 00:28:57,690
of how we're processing paperwork just for graduation.

708
00:28:57,690 --> 00:28:59,370
So I would say, on a side note,

709
00:28:59,370 --> 00:29:02,760
that's been an amazing thing, to be able to connect

710
00:29:02,760 --> 00:29:05,070
with all of the different departments on our campus

711
00:29:05,070 --> 00:29:08,070
that connect students to completing their program,

712
00:29:08,070 --> 00:29:10,980
and we've been able to collaborate

713
00:29:10,980 --> 00:29:15,390
and take out multiple forms,

714
00:29:15,390 --> 00:29:18,060
where now it's just one single form.

715
00:29:18,060 --> 00:29:20,553
And so now we're able to use that one single form

716
00:29:20,553 --> 00:29:24,120
that now combines multiple forms that we had do in the past

717
00:29:24,120 --> 00:29:25,620
within this integration

718
00:29:25,620 --> 00:29:27,630
so that all of the information is on one page.

719
00:29:27,630 --> 00:29:30,090
And that took our campus to really be on board

720
00:29:30,090 --> 00:29:32,700
with how can we better support our internal process

721
00:29:32,700 --> 00:29:33,993
so that this could work.

722
00:29:35,070 --> 00:29:38,100
It goes both ways. And so yes, the technology helps.

723
00:29:38,100 --> 00:29:39,300
But we also have to be open

724
00:29:39,300 --> 00:29:42,270
to how do we envision this change

725
00:29:42,270 --> 00:29:43,860
and what do we need to do as a campus

726
00:29:43,860 --> 00:29:45,510
to kind of think about what we can do

727
00:29:45,510 --> 00:29:47,820
just within our daily work.

728
00:29:47,820 --> 00:29:49,980
And so with that, the other piece

729
00:29:49,980 --> 00:29:51,510
that we were able to really think about, too,

730
00:29:51,510 --> 00:29:54,630
is integrating CPL, credit for prior learning.

731
00:29:54,630 --> 00:29:55,830
So, one of the things

732
00:29:55,830 --> 00:29:59,850
that we built into this process, as well, is...

733
00:29:59,850 --> 00:30:01,860
For our apprenticeship students,

734
00:30:01,860 --> 00:30:03,060
one of the things that happens

735
00:30:03,060 --> 00:30:08,060
is that the training trust does CPL,

736
00:30:08,070 --> 00:30:10,680
and they've been doing CPL for a long time,

737
00:30:10,680 --> 00:30:12,840
way longer than we even been thinking about it.

738
00:30:12,840 --> 00:30:15,570
When a student goes into a carpentry training trust

739
00:30:15,570 --> 00:30:16,980
to be indentured,

740
00:30:16,980 --> 00:30:19,290
they are evaluated on their previous work experience,

741
00:30:19,290 --> 00:30:22,803
and they can skip levels, right, and then indenture out.

742
00:30:23,670 --> 00:30:27,030
We didn't have a process in place for those gaps,

743
00:30:27,030 --> 00:30:28,770
when the students didn't have that coursework

744
00:30:28,770 --> 00:30:29,760
when they indentured higher.

745
00:30:29,760 --> 00:30:31,590
So now within this system

746
00:30:31,590 --> 00:30:33,600
and now within this integration with N2N,

747
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:38,100
we're able to build, within that, again, singlized form, CPL

748
00:30:38,100 --> 00:30:40,110
into this process, so it's really exciting we're able

749
00:30:40,110 --> 00:30:42,900
to kind of, again, bring in a lot of internal processes

750
00:30:42,900 --> 00:30:46,020
plus build this through, so we're excited.

751
00:30:46,020 --> 00:30:47,880
The other piece that's really cool about this,

752
00:30:47,880 --> 00:30:49,890
that was probably the best thing

753
00:30:49,890 --> 00:30:53,010
that Kiran showed us, at N2N, recently...

754
00:30:53,010 --> 00:30:55,440
His team was able to show us that our internal form

755
00:30:55,440 --> 00:30:58,740
that we created to graduate students that minimized...

756
00:30:58,740 --> 00:31:01,880
The system is actually able to automate all

757
00:31:01,880 --> 00:31:04,470
of the fields and information within that PDF

758
00:31:04,470 --> 00:31:07,830
so that everything is be able to be verified and checked

759
00:31:07,830 --> 00:31:09,600
but can be sent over

760
00:31:09,600 --> 00:31:11,310
directly to our admissions and records team

761
00:31:11,310 --> 00:31:12,960
once our counselors have reviewed it

762
00:31:12,960 --> 00:31:17,220
so taking down a lot of not only processing steps

763
00:31:17,220 --> 00:31:19,200
but people that have to kind of stop

764
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:20,790
and check each of these different levels.

765
00:31:20,790 --> 00:31:22,830
So we're really excited.

766
00:31:22,830 --> 00:31:25,710
Stay tuned as we, you know, have the opportunity

767
00:31:25,710 --> 00:31:27,930
to kind of see what the outcomes will be

768
00:31:27,930 --> 00:31:30,000
and what we're able to build this from moving forward,

769
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,190
but I think a lotta of great implications

770
00:31:32,190 --> 00:31:33,440
for everyone in the room.

771
00:31:34,860 --> 00:31:36,993
- Thank you. Thank you all for sharing.

772
00:31:37,920 --> 00:31:40,560
I hope this is starting to give you ideas of where some

773
00:31:40,560 --> 00:31:43,680
of your pain points and processes

774
00:31:43,680 --> 00:31:46,620
might benefit from some AI solutions,

775
00:31:46,620 --> 00:31:47,547
but I wanna throw this question out

776
00:31:47,547 --> 00:31:51,000
because some of you touched on it a little bit.

777
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:55,890
We all know at our institutions that change is hard.

778
00:31:55,890 --> 00:31:58,560
It's hard to get people to do things differently.

779
00:31:58,560 --> 00:32:00,760
Are you from an institution where it's easy?

780
00:32:02,367 --> 00:32:05,343
Oh, I was hoping we'd have some examples.

781
00:32:06,510 --> 00:32:08,790
Change is hard, so I'm gonna ask...

782
00:32:08,790 --> 00:32:10,620
I'm just gonna throw it out to the panelists.

783
00:32:10,620 --> 00:32:12,120
Knowing that change is hard

784
00:32:12,120 --> 00:32:14,100
and even though you're doing something

785
00:32:14,100 --> 00:32:16,620
or trying to deploy a solution

786
00:32:16,620 --> 00:32:18,030
or develop a solution

787
00:32:18,030 --> 00:32:20,970
that will help build capacity and improve workloads,

788
00:32:20,970 --> 00:32:23,310
what challenges or roadblocks did you face?

789
00:32:23,310 --> 00:32:26,673
And how did you overcome that to help move people along?

